Lab: Manage IaaS VM in Azure

Objective

Create a Virtual Machine in Azure under the correct Subscription and Resource group. 

Environment

Tools Used

Microsoft Azure Portal
Azure Resource Groups
Azure Subscription Management
Azure Virtual Machine

Operating System

Microsoft Azure

Skills Practiced

Cloud Administration
Resource Management
Azure Portal Navigation
Cloud Infrastructure Configuration
Virtual Machine Deployment

Actions Performed

1. Logged into MS Azure Portal.
2. On the Azure Home Page, the Resource Group is listed under resources. 
3. Under the Azure Services is Virtual Machine. I opened this page to configure the Virtual Machine.  
4. The new page shows no previously created VMs. I clicked on Create Azure virtual machine.
5. In the Create a virtual machine page, I corrected the subscription and verified the Resource group name. 
6. In Instance Details, I provided a VM name, provided the correct region, specified the image required for the VM, and specified the size of the VM.
7. Under Administrator Account, I added the username and password.
8. I then moved to the Disks page and selected Standard HDD with Delete with the VM box selected. 
9. I reviewed the remaining options and reviewed my settings on the Review + create page to verify everything was correct before creating the machine. Once verified, I created the VM. 
10. Documented findings and ended lab

Commands Used

None

Verification

After creating the VM, Azure sent me to a page showing the in-progress deployment of the VM. Once complete, it displayed completion with the corresponding subscription and resource group. Also, going to the Azure Home Portal, I was able to see and confirm the status by clicking the VM hyperlink. 

Issues Encountered

None

Resolution

Successfully created and verified the Azure Virtual Machine within the selected subscription and region. Confirmed the Virtual Machine was available. 

Key Takeaways

Resource groups help organize and manage related cloud resources.
VM sizing impacts performance and cost to an organization.
Reviewing configurations before deployment helps prevent resource misconfiguration. 
 
